"Deterministic, bounded native-parameter correction for two linear readout interfaces. propose_linear solves a ridge-regularized least-squares problem in the native weight space using the exact native forward equation (baseline + features@(newweights-weights)), with per-output-group solves when shared=False and a single shared solve when shared=True. The ridge penalty is applied to the delta (newweights - weights) so the correction is bounded and shrinks toward the original parameters; a small diagonal stabilizer is added for numerical safety. No intercept offsets are added because features already contain native intercept coordinates. propose_rna optionally probes host-supplied hidden directions with two-sided finite differences of the training-feedback residual, builds a small regularized linear model in the K hidden-direction coefficients, and selects a bounded step along the best direction; it then calls native_state at the perturbed hidden vector to obtain the updated features/weights and applies propose_linear for the readout correction. If hidden probing is disabled or yields no improvement, it falls back to output-only readout correction at the base vector. All operations are closed-form (no iterative optimization), deterministic, and leave inputs unchanged. Recipes fix the ridge strengths, hidden step radius, and probe count so the same code is reused across feedback partitions and seeds without query-dependent tuning. Technical repairs: (a) removed the forbidden numpy import; np is supplied by the host. (b) defined base_err from the base residual before constructing the hidden-direction linear model. (c) residual_at treats native_state baseline as the complete current native prediction, so the predicted value is baseline + features@(newweights-weights) with newweights equal to the current weights (i.e. baseline itself at the probed vector); the residual is computed against target using that exact native forward. (d) propose_linear uses an algebraically equivalent small-sample ridge solve: since n<=16, we solve in the n-dimensional dual space (F F^T + lam*tr*I) instead of forming the DxD system, then map back via F^T, avoiding repeated dense 514x514 solves per output while preserving the same ridge estimator and regularization. (e) _ridge_solve now chooses the algebraically equivalent primal or dual expression based on the smaller of the actual design matrix row count m and column count d, so the shared scGPT fit (m=8192, d=1026) uses the primal d-dimensional system rather than an 8192x8192 dual; the unshared per-output fits (m<=16, d=514) use the small row system. The regularization scale trace(X.T@X) is computed as sum(X*X) without materializing the large Gram. All recipes, lambdas, objectives, bounded updates, hidden mechanisms and other numerical behavior are preserved."
